# Source for q/spi-optimize.js: Optimize sonographic images (SPI domain 3): practical controls and trade-offs.
I="Instrumentation"; R="Resolution"
Q=[
# --- Gain and TGC scenarios ---
(I,"The whole image is too dark, including near and far fields. The first adjustment is to:","Increase overall receiver gain",["Increase output power to maximum","Decrease depth","Add a focal zone"],"Gain brightens all echoes equally without increasing patient exposure.","F"),
(I,"The near field is too bright while the far field looks fine. The best correction is:","Lower the near-field TGC sliders",["Lower overall gain","Raise output power","Raise dynamic range"],"TGC adjusts brightness at specific depths.","F"),
(I,"The far field is too dark while the near field looks fine. The best correction is:","Raise the far-field TGC sliders",["Raise overall gain","Lower the near-field TGC","Decrease frequency only"],"If the far field still lacks echoes, a lower frequency may be needed for penetration.","F"),
(I,"Cardiac chambers are filled with fine grey noise. The best correction is:","Reduce gain (or raise reject)",["Increase gain","Increase output power","Widen the sector"],"Blood should look black at proper gain settings.","F"),
(I,"TGC is also called:","Depth gain compensation (DGC) or swept gain",["Dynamic range","Persistence","Output power"],"Some systems label it STC (sensitivity time control).","F"),
(I,"Changing TGC affects:","Received echo amplification by depth, not patient exposure",["Transmitted intensity","Frequency","Frame rate"],"Only output power and exposure time change patient exposure.","F"),
(I,"After changing the depth setting, the sonographer should recheck:","TGC and focal zone position",["The patient's ID only","Color map","Probe frequency"],"Depth changes move structures relative to TGC sliders and focus.","F"),
# --- Depth, zoom, focus ---
(I,"A small structure fills only the top third of the screen. The best first step is to:","Reduce depth so the structure fills more of the display",["Increase depth","Add more focal zones","Increase gain"],"Reducing depth also raises the PRF and frame rate.","F"),
(I,"Reducing imaging depth improves:","Frame rate and temporal resolution",["Penetration","Axial resolution","Elevational resolution"],"Less listening time per line means more frames per second.","F"),
(I,"The transmit focus should be placed:","At or just below the structure of interest",["Always at the top of the image","Always at the bottom of the image","Far below the area of interest"],"This narrows the beam where detail matters most.","F"),
(I,"Using one focal zone instead of four will:","Raise the frame rate",["Lower the frame rate","Improve lateral resolution at all depths","Change the transmitted frequency"],"Each focal zone requires its own transmitted pulse per line.","F"),
(I,"Write magnification of a region of interest:","Acquires new data with more lines and pixels in that area, improving detail",["Only enlarges existing pixels","Works on frozen images only","Lowers spatial resolution"],"It is a pre-processing function done during live imaging.","F"),
(I,"Read magnification of a frozen image:","Enlarges stored pixels without adding new detail",["Improves spatial resolution","Rescans the region","Increases frame rate"],"Images can look blocky because the same pixels are just made bigger.","F"),
(I,"Write zoom can improve frame rate because:","The machine only scans the smaller region of interest",["It increases depth","It adds focal zones","It raises output power"],"Fewer lines and shallower depth may be needed for the zoomed area.","C"),
# --- Frame rate calculations and trade-offs ---
(R,"Maximum frame rate ≈ 77,000 ÷ (depth in cm × lines per frame × focal zones). For 10 cm, 128 lines and 1 focal zone, frame rate is about:","60 frames per second",["600 frames per second","6 frames per second","30 frames per second"],"77,000 ÷ 1,280 ≈ 60 fps.","C"),
(R,"Using the same settings (10 cm, 128 lines) with 2 focal zones, frame rate becomes about:","30 frames per second",["60 frames per second","120 frames per second","15 frames per second"],"Two focal zones double the time per frame.","C"),
(R,"Depth is increased from 8 cm to 16 cm with all else the same. Frame rate:","Halves",["Doubles","Stays the same","Quadruples"],"Each line takes twice as long to listen for echoes.","F"),
(R,"Doubling line density with the same sector width will:","Improve lateral detail but halve the frame rate",["Raise the frame rate","Lower lateral detail","Change the axial resolution"],"Each frame needs twice as many lines.","F"),
(R,"Which change does NOT affect frame rate?","Overall receiver gain",["Imaging depth","Sector width","Number of focal zones"],"Gain only changes the brightness of received signals.","F"),
(R,"Frame rate 40 frames per second means the time for each frame is:","25 ms",["40 ms","4 ms","250 ms"],"Frame time = 1 ÷ frame rate = 1 ÷ 40 s = 25 ms.","F"),
(R,"Temporal resolution worsens when:","Frame rate falls",["Frame rate rises","Depth is reduced","Sector is narrowed"],"Fewer frames per second means rapid motion is captured less accurately.","F"),
(R,"Which combination gives the highest frame rate?","Shallow depth, narrow sector, single focal zone",["Deep, wide sector, many focal zones","Deep, narrow sector, many focal zones","Shallow, wide sector, many focal zones"],"Each choice reduces the time needed to build one frame.","F"),
(R,"Turning on spatial compounding usually:","Lowers frame rate",["Raises frame rate","Has no effect on frame rate","Improves axial resolution only"],"Several steered frames are combined for each displayed image.","F"),
# --- Frequency and harmonics ---
(I,"For a slender patient with superficial structures, the best transducer choice is:","A higher frequency for better resolution",["A lower frequency for penetration","The lowest possible frequency","CW Doppler probe"],"Use the highest frequency that still reaches the depth needed.","F"),
(I,"For a large patient with poor far-field penetration, the best change is:","Lower the transducer frequency",["Raise the frequency","Increase sector width","Add focal zones"],"Lower frequencies attenuate less, improving penetration at the cost of resolution.","F"),
(I,"Tissue harmonic imaging helps most in:","Technically difficult patients, by reducing clutter and improving borders",["Every Doppler measurement","Reducing frame rate","Measuring valve thickness precisely"],"Harmonics reduce near-field reverberation and side-lobe artifacts.","F"),
(I,"A disadvantage of tissue harmonic imaging is that it:","Makes thin structures such as valves look thicker and reduces axial resolution somewhat",["Increases near-field clutter","Removes all artifacts","Raises patient exposure dramatically"],"The harmonic pulse is longer relative to its frequency band.","C"),
(I,"Frequency compounding combines:","Images made at different frequencies to reduce speckle",["Images from different angles","Doppler and B-mode","3D volumes"],"Spatial compounding uses different steering angles instead.","C"),
# --- Dynamic range, processing ---
(I,"An image looks washed out and grey with poor contrast. The best change is:","Reduce the dynamic range (more compression)",["Increase the dynamic range","Increase gain","Lower frequency"],"A narrower dynamic range makes the image higher contrast.","F"),
(I,"An image looks harsh, mostly black and white, and subtle tissue texture is lost. The best change is:","Increase the dynamic range",["Reduce the dynamic range","Increase reject","Lower gain"],"A wider dynamic range shows more shades of grey.","F"),
(I,"Raising the reject level too high can:","Remove weak true echoes, such as subtle thrombus or tissue texture",["Add noise","Raise frame rate","Cause aliasing"],"Reject removes low-level signals, real or noise.","F"),
(I,"Grayscale maps (post-processing curves) change:","How echo strengths are assigned to shades of grey on the display",["The transmitted power","The frequency","The depth"],"Different maps highlight different tissue contrast.","F"),
(I,"B-color (colorized B-mode) is used to:","Assign colors instead of grey shades to improve perceived contrast",["Show blood flow","Show Doppler velocity","Measure elasticity"],"It is a post-processing display option; it carries no flow information.","F"),
(I,"Speckle reduction imaging works by:","Processing the image to smooth speckle while preserving edges",["Lowering output power","Raising PRF","Using CW Doppler"],"It improves appearance but heavy smoothing can hide fine detail.","F"),
(I,"Edge enhancement makes images look sharper but can:","Exaggerate noise and create artificial borders",["Lower frame rate greatly","Remove shadows","Cause aliasing"],"Use moderate settings.","C"),
(I,"Persistence should usually be set low for echocardiography because:","Heart structures move quickly and averaging blurs them",["It raises frame rate","It increases penetration","It improves Doppler"],"Higher persistence suits slow-moving abdominal structures.","F"),
(I,"Before scanning, monitor brightness and contrast should be adjusted for:","The room lighting, so that grey levels are displayed correctly",["The patient's size","The Doppler angle","The probe frequency"],"Badly adjusted monitors lead to wrong gain choices.","F"),
# --- Output power ---
(I,"Increasing output power improves an image mainly by:","Improving signal-to-noise ratio so weak deep echoes stand out",["Improving axial resolution","Raising frame rate","Narrowing the beam"],"Use it only after gain and TGC are optimized (ALARA).","F"),
(I,"Changing output power affects:","MI, TI and the brightness of the image",["Only image brightness","Only frame rate","Only the Doppler angle"],"It is the main control that changes patient exposure.","F"),
(I,"An image is too bright overall. Following ALARA, the best fix is to:","Lower the output power",["Lower the receiver gain","Increase the depth","Raise the dynamic range"],"Reducing output lowers both brightness and patient exposure.","C"),
# --- M-mode ---
(I,"M-mode is best for:","Measuring rapid motion and timing along one line",["Measuring flow direction","Displaying a 2D slice","Measuring velocity with Doppler"],"It samples thousands of times per second.","F"),
(I,"For accurate M-mode measurements, the cursor should be:","Perpendicular to the structure being measured",["Parallel to the structure","At any angle","At 45° to the structure"],"Oblique cuts overestimate dimensions.","F"),
(I,"Anatomic (steerable) M-mode lets the sonographer:","Place the M-mode line in any direction on a stored 2D image",["Use only the central scan line","Measure Doppler velocity","Make 3D images"],"It helps when the true M-mode line can't be aligned perpendicular to a wall.","C"),
(I,"M-mode sweep speed affects:","How spread out the motion appears over time",["The sampling rate along the line","The depth","The transmitted frequency"],"Faster sweeps are used for precise timing; slower ones show more beats.","F"),
(I,"Color M-mode is useful for:","Timing flow events, such as the duration of a regurgitant jet",["Measuring 3D volumes","Showing wall texture only","Replacing CW Doppler"],"It combines high temporal resolution with flow direction.","F"),
# --- 3D / EFOV ---
(I,"Extended field of view (panoramic) imaging works by:","Stitching together frames as the transducer slides along the skin",["Using a wider sector","Increasing depth","Using a lower frequency"],"Moving too fast or unevenly can distort measurements.","F"),
(I,"Multiplanar reconstruction (MPR) in 3D imaging displays:","Three perpendicular 2D slices from a volume",["A surface rendering only","A Doppler spectrum","An M-mode line"],"The sonographer can scroll through the volume in any plane.","F"),
(I,"Surface rendering in 3D imaging shows:","The outer surface of structures, such as a valve viewed 'en face'",["Internal Doppler velocities","Only grey-scale slices","Time-motion data"],"The mitral valve 'surgeon's view' from the LA is a common example.","F"),
(I,"4D ultrasound is:","Real-time 3D imaging (3D plus time)",["Four 2D images side by side","3D with color only","Doppler in four directions"],"Volume rate is lower than 2D frame rate.","F"),
(I,"3D volume rate improves when:","The volume size is reduced or fewer beats are stitched together",["The volume is enlarged","Depth is increased","Line density is increased"],"Stitching multiple beats gives larger volumes at higher rates but risks artifacts.","C"),
(I,"Matrix array transducers are needed for:","Real-time electronic 3D imaging",["Single-line M-mode","CW Doppler","Mechanical sector scanning"],"Thousands of elements steer the beam in both lateral and elevational planes.","F"),
# --- Elastography (in SPI outline under advanced techniques) ---
(I,"Shear wave elastography measures tissue stiffness by:","Tracking the speed of shear waves through tissue",["Measuring Doppler shift of blood","Using harmonic frequencies only","Measuring attenuation only"],"Stiffer tissue carries shear waves faster. Results are given in m/s or kPa.","C"),
(I,"In strain elastography, stiffer tissue:","Deforms less under compression",["Deforms more under compression","Shows no difference","Appears anechoic always"],"Strain is displayed as a color map comparing tissue deformation.","C"),
# --- Resolution in practice ---
(R,"Two thin layers of the aortic wall are seen as one line. To separate them, the sonographer should:","Use a higher frequency to shorten the pulse",["Increase gain","Increase depth","Add persistence"],"Axial resolution depends on spatial pulse length.","F"),
(R,"Two small side-by-side structures at 12 cm depth appear merged. The best change is:","Move the focus to 12 cm",["Lower the frequency","Increase gain","Increase persistence"],"Lateral resolution is best at the focus.","F"),
(R,"A fluid-filled chamber shows echoes that come from neighboring tissue above and below the slice. This is a limitation of:","Elevational resolution",["Axial resolution","Temporal resolution","Contrast resolution"],"1.5D and matrix arrays reduce slice thickness.","F"),
(R,"Valve leaflet motion looks jerky and incomplete on 2D. The best improvement is to:","Increase frame rate by narrowing the sector and reducing depth",["Add focal zones","Increase persistence","Increase line density"],"Higher frame rates capture brief motion.","F"),
(R,"Subtle differences between normal and infarcted myocardium are hard to see. Which resolution is involved?","Contrast resolution",["Axial resolution","Temporal resolution","Elevational resolution"],"Dynamic range, harmonics and processing affect contrast resolution.","F"),
(R,"Which control improves contrast resolution in the scan converter?","More bits per pixel",["Fewer pixels","Higher frame rate","Lower frequency"],"More bits allow more shades of grey.","F"),
(R,"Spatial resolution of the displayed image improves with:","More pixels per inch",["More bits per pixel","Higher gain","Lower frame rate only"],"Pixel density affects detail; bits per pixel affect grey shades.","F"),
(R,"Improving lateral resolution with a larger aperture works best for:","Deep structures, where a larger aperture keeps the beam narrow",["Superficial structures right under the probe","Doppler only","M-mode only"],"Arrays dynamically increase the active aperture with depth.","C"),
(R,"Dynamic aperture means:","The number of active receiving elements increases for deeper echoes",["The transducer changes size physically","The PRF changes with depth","The frequency changes with depth"],"This keeps the beam narrow at depth for better lateral resolution.","C"),
(R,"A pulse with fewer cycles has:","A wider bandwidth",["A narrower bandwidth","A higher Q factor","Worse axial resolution"],"Short pulses contain many frequencies.","F"),
(R,"Which statement about resolution and frequency is correct?","Higher frequency improves axial and lateral resolution but reduces penetration",["Lower frequency improves all resolution","Frequency doesn't affect resolution","Higher frequency improves penetration"],"Always choose the highest frequency that reaches the target.","F"),
(R,"An image built with more scan lines per degree of sector has:","Better lateral detail between lines",["Better axial resolution","Higher frame rate","Lower patient exposure"],"Gaps between widely spaced lines lose lateral detail.","F"),
(R,"Coded excitation can improve penetration without losing axial resolution because:","Long coded pulses are compressed back into short pulses on receive",["It uses lower frequencies","It reduces depth","It adds focal zones"],"This improves signal-to-noise ratio at depth.","C"),
(R,"Why does harmonic imaging improve lateral resolution?","The harmonic beam is narrower, formed mainly in the central high-intensity region",["Harmonics have longer wavelengths","Harmonics travel faster","Harmonics increase frame rate"],"Weak off-axis energy creates few harmonics.","C"),
(R,"Increasing the number of transmit focal zones improves:","Lateral resolution over a longer range of depths",["Axial resolution","Frame rate","Penetration"],"The trade-off is a lower frame rate.","F"),
(R,"Which resolution can only be improved by changing transducer or frequency, not by focusing?","Axial resolution",["Lateral resolution","Elevational resolution on a matrix probe","Temporal resolution"],"Pulse length is set by the transducer's frequency and damping.","F"),
(R,"A sonographer needs both a high frame rate and good lateral resolution over a large depth. The best compromise is to:","Use one focal zone at the area of most interest and a narrow sector",["Use five focal zones and a wide sector","Increase persistence","Lower the frequency"],"Every resolution improvement has a cost; choose what matters for the question being asked.","C"),
]
